"""The names of the things `{{ app_name }}` says happened. Names only.

`<app>.<noun>.<past-tense verb>`, one constant per name, declared here and never
written as a literal at a call site (house rule 16):

    THING_CREATED = "{{ app_name }}.thing.created"

Three reasons, all of them practical:

* an event name is a PUBLISHED CONTRACT. A webhook subscriber matches on it and
  a rename breaks every integration silently, so it has one definition;
* the grammar is much easier to keep in one file per app than spread across
  call sites;
* `manage.py introspect` reports this file, so a client can read the list of
  events it may subscribe to without reading the source.

Past tense, always: an event states that something HAS happened.
`{{ app_name }}.thing.create` would be an instruction, and an instruction is a
service call. `services.py` publishes these with `emit()` inside the write's
own transaction.
"""

from __future__ import annotations
